{"record": "turn", "turn": 4, "seconds": 3.625, "response_model": "claude-sonnet-5-5", "stop_reason": "tool_use", "stop_details": null, "blocks": ["tool_use"], "usage": {"cache_creation": {"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens": 0, "ephemeral_5m_input_tokens": 5475}, "cache_creation_input_tokens": 5475, "cache_read_input_tokens": 6931, "inference_geo": "global", "input_tokens": 2, "output_tokens": 447, "output_tokens_details": {"thinking_tokens": 0}, "service_tier": "standard", "speed": "standard"}, "tools": [{"id": "toolu_01U7ZnmcCZpZNExgdSB3WdgE", "name": "submit_answer", "arguments": {"answer": "The paper says annihilation to on-shell hh gives a good fit \"so long as h is produced close to rest\". The best-fit values in Table 1 are m_χ ≈ 125.7 (+3.1/−0) GeV, ⟨σv⟩ ≈ 5.33 ± 0.68 ×10⁻²⁶ cm³ s⁻¹, χ²_min = 29.5 and p-value = 0.13. For comparison, b b̄ gives m_χ ≈ 48.7 GeV.\n\nThe paper explains the good fit by treating hh as a cascade annihilation. Each h decays mainly to b b̄, and each b then has energy m_h/2. The b quarks are therefore boosted, which lets the DM be heavier than in direct channels while still matching the spectrum. The paper says this is why the DM mass is \"over twice as heavy\" as for other channels and the fit is still good.", "found": true, "evidence": [{"arxiv_id": "1411.4647v2", "section_id": "4.1"}, {"arxiv_id": "1411.4647v2", "section_id": "4.4"}]}, "status": "answer", "error_kind": null, "seconds": 0.0}]}
